{
  "id": "sat-data",
  "chapterNumber": "5",
  "title": {
    "en": "Problem-Solving &amp; Data Analysis",
    "ar": null
  },
  "chapterTag": {
    "variant": "category",
    "className": "sat-domain-tag",
    "emoji": "📌",
    "categoryPrefix": "Domain:",
    "category": "Problem-Solving &amp; Data Analysis",
    "topics": [
      "Ratios, rates, proportional relationships",
      "Percentages",
      "One-variable data (center, spread)",
      "Two-variable data",
      "Probability &amp; conditional probability"
    ]
  },
  "content": {
    "blocks": [
      {
        "kind": "text",
        "body": {
          "en": "The most word-heavy domain: ratios, rates and percentages, units, statistics, probability, and reading models from data.",
          "ar": null
        }
      },
      {
        "kind": "callout",
        "style": "thm",
        "label": {
          "en": "▲ Reference — Rates, percent &amp; stats",
          "ar": null
        },
        "body": {
          "en": "Percent of: \\( \\tfrac{p}{100}\\times N \\); a unit rate divides to “per one”. Mean is total over count; the <strong>line of best fit</strong> models a linear trend in a scatterplot.",
          "ar": null
        }
      },
      {
        "kind": "text",
        "body": {
          "en": "Slide the trend line's slope to shrink the total squared residual (SSR). The smallest SSR marks the line of best fit.",
          "ar": null
        }
      },
      {
        "kind": "explorer",
        "title": {
          "en": "⊙ Explorer · Line of best fit",
          "ar": null
        },
        "canvasId": "satFitCanvas",
        "ariaLabel": {
          "en": "A scatterplot with an adjustable trend line and its squared residuals",
          "ar": null
        },
        "dataPanel": {
          "btnId": "satFitDataBtn",
          "panelId": "satFitDataPanel",
          "descId": "satFitDataDesc",
          "rowsId": "satFitDataRows",
          "caption": {
            "en": "Each data point, the line's predicted value, and the residual",
            "ar": null
          },
          "columns": [
            {
              "en": "x",
              "ar": null
            },
            {
              "en": "actual y",
              "ar": null
            },
            {
              "en": "predicted y",
              "ar": null
            },
            {
              "en": "residual",
              "ar": null
            },
            {
              "en": "residual²",
              "ar": null
            }
          ]
        },
        "controls": [
          {
            "id": "satFitSlope",
            "label": {
              "en": "Trend slope =",
              "ar": null
            },
            "min": 0,
            "max": 2,
            "value": 0.5,
            "step": 0.05,
            "valueDisplayId": "satFitSlopeV",
            "ariaLabel": {
              "en": "Trend slope",
              "ar": null
            }
          }
        ],
        "readouts": [
          {
            "id": "satFitSSR",
            "label": {
              "en": "sum of squared residuals",
              "ar": null
            },
            "colorClass": "amber",
            "value": "—"
          }
        ],
        "note": {
          "en": "The line pivots through the data's centre; the best-fit slope here is about \\( 0.95 \\).",
          "ar": null
        }
      },
      {
        "kind": "cards",
        "items": [
          {
            "label": {
              "en": "Percent of",
              "ar": null
            },
            "value": {
              "en": "\\( \\tfrac{p}{100}\\times N \\)",
              "ar": null
            }
          },
          {
            "label": {
              "en": "Unit rate",
              "ar": null
            },
            "value": {
              "en": "per one unit",
              "ar": null
            }
          },
          {
            "label": {
              "en": "Mean",
              "ar": null
            },
            "value": {
              "en": "\\( \\dfrac{\\text{sum}}{\\text{count}} \\)",
              "ar": null
            }
          },
          {
            "label": {
              "en": "Probability",
              "ar": null
            },
            "value": {
              "en": "\\( \\dfrac{\\text{favourable}}{\\text{total}} \\)",
              "ar": null
            }
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "kind": "callout",
        "style": "example",
        "label": {
          "en": "Worked example 5.A",
          "ar": null
        },
        "body": {
          "en": "<p>Find \\( 20\\% \\) of \\( 150 \\).</p>\\[ 0.20\\times150=30. \\]",
          "ar": null
        }
      },
      {
        "kind": "callout",
        "style": "example",
        "label": {
          "en": "Worked example 5.B",
          "ar": null
        },
        "body": {
          "en": "<p>A $40 shirt is marked down \\( 30\\% \\). Find the sale price.</p>\\[ 40\\times0.70=\\$28. \\]",
          "ar": null
        }
      },
      {
        "kind": "callout",
        "style": "def",
        "label": {
          "en": "▲ Measures of Center &amp; Spread",
          "ar": null
        },
        "body": {
          "en": "<strong>Mean</strong> (average) is sensitive to outliers; <strong>median</strong> (middle value, sorted) is resistant. <strong>Range</strong> = max − min. <strong>IQR</strong> = Q3 − Q1 (middle 50 % of data; also resistant to outliers). <strong>Standard deviation</strong>: the SAT never asks you to compute it — it asks you to <em>compare</em> or <em>interpret</em> it. A larger SD means greater spread around the mean.",
          "ar": null
        }
      },
      {
        "kind": "cards",
        "items": [
          {
            "label": {
              "en": "Mean",
              "ar": null
            },
            "value": {
              "en": "\\(\\bar x=\\dfrac{\\sum x_i}{n}\\) — sensitive to outliers",
              "ar": null
            }
          },
          {
            "label": {
              "en": "Median",
              "ar": null
            },
            "value": {
              "en": "Middle value (sorted); avg of two middles if even",
              "ar": null
            }
          },
          {
            "label": {
              "en": "IQR",
              "ar": null
            },
            "value": {
              "en": "\\(Q_3-Q_1\\) — resistant to outliers",
              "ar": null
            }
          },
          {
            "label": {
              "en": "Std deviation",
              "ar": null
            },
            "value": {
              "en": "Spread from mean; larger SD = more spread",
              "ar": null
            }
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "kind": "callout",
        "style": "example",
        "label": {
          "en": "Worked example 5.C — Median &amp; IQR",
          "ar": null
        },
        "body": {
          "en": "<p>Data: 3, 7, 8, 12, 14, 18, 21.</p><p><strong>Median</strong> = 4th value = <strong>12</strong>. &nbsp;Q1 = median of {3,7,8} = 7. &nbsp;Q3 = median of {14,18,21} = 18.</p>\\[\\text{IQR} = 18-7 = 11.\\]",
          "ar": null
        }
      },
      {
        "kind": "callout",
        "style": "def",
        "label": {
          "en": "▲ Sampling, Inference &amp; Margin of Error",
          "ar": null
        },
        "body": {
          "en": "A <strong>random sample</strong> allows generalisation to the population it was drawn from — no further. <strong>Voluntary-response</strong> and <strong>convenience samples</strong> are biased. <strong>Observational studies</strong> can show association only; establishing <strong>causation</strong> requires a <strong>randomised controlled experiment</strong> (random assignment to groups). A <strong>margin of error</strong> creates a confidence interval: estimate ± margin of error.",
          "ar": null
        }
      },
      {
        "kind": "cards",
        "items": [
          {
            "label": {
              "en": "Confidence interval",
              "ar": null
            },
            "value": {
              "en": "estimate \\(\\pm\\) margin of error",
              "ar": null
            }
          },
          {
            "label": {
              "en": "Association",
              "ar": null
            },
            "value": {
              "en": "Observational study (no causation)",
              "ar": null
            }
          },
          {
            "label": {
              "en": "Causation",
              "ar": null
            },
            "value": {
              "en": "Randomised experiment only",
              "ar": null
            }
          },
          {
            "label": {
              "en": "Generalise to …",
              "ar": null
            },
            "value": {
              "en": "Only the population sampled",
              "ar": null
            }
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "kind": "callout",
        "style": "example",
        "label": {
          "en": "Worked example 5.D — Margin of error",
          "ar": null
        },
        "body": {
          "en": "<p>A poll of 600 voters finds 54 % support Candidate A, margin of error ±3 %.</p><p>True support is estimated to be between <strong>51 % and 57 %</strong>. Since the entire interval exceeds 50 %, the data support a majority — but only for the population sampled.</p>",
          "ar": null
        }
      },
      {
        "kind": "practiceProblem",
        "question": {
          "en": "<strong>SAT-style:</strong> A researcher randomly assigns 50 students to a tutoring programme and 50 to a control group, then compares test scores. Which conclusion is best supported if the tutored group scores significantly higher?<br><br>(A) Tutoring <em>causes</em> higher scores.<br>(B) There is an <em>association</em> but causation cannot be determined.<br>(C) Results apply to all students nationwide.<br>(D) The sample size is too small to draw any conclusion.",
          "ar": null
        },
        "solution": {
          "en": "<strong>(A).</strong> Random assignment to groups (not just observation) allows a causal conclusion. Results generalise to the population sampled, not all students, so (C) is wrong.",
          "ar": null
        }
      }
    ]
  },
  "quizWidget": {
    "enabled": true
  }
}
